---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Copilot in Plain English | WorkSmart-AI',
  description:
    'Every term you will hear in a Copilot session, explained once, without the marketing. Written for the person using it, not the person configuring it.',
  canonical: '/copilot-glossary/',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <article class="copilot-glossary">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">WorkSmart-AI &middot; Copilot curriculum</p>
      <h1>Copilot in plain English</h1>
      <p class="stand">
        Every term you will hear in a Copilot session, explained once, without the marketing. Written for the person
        using it, not the person configuring it.
      </p>
      <p class="stand sub">
        Search or filter below. The tag is an ordinary category &mdash; licences, your work, where you use it, prompts,
        agents, trust, your organisation &mdash; not a Microsoft product name. Every entry links to the Microsoft page
        it was checked against on 20 August 2026, or says plainly that no such page exists.
      </p>

      <section class="core">
        <h2>If you only learn four</h2>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>Agent</dt><dd>
              <b>Who</b> does the work &mdash; a named Copilot with a job, rules, and a fixed set of places it may read.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Skill</dt><dd>
              <b>What</b> that agent can do &mdash; one named task, described once and reused. Copilot Studio has a Skills
              panel for exactly this.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Action</dt><dd>
              <b>What it can reach</b> &mdash; the ability to touch a real system: read a library, send a mail, update a
              list.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Workflow</dt><dd>
              <b>When, and in what order</b> &mdash; steps that fire on a trigger and run without you sitting there.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <div class="controls">
        <label for="q" class="hidden">Search the glossary</label>
        <input id="q" class="search" type="search" placeholder="Search a term, or what it does…" autocomplete="off" />
        <div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter by part of the picture">
          <button type="button" data-cat="all" aria-pressed="true">All</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="licences" aria-pressed="false">Licences</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="work" aria-pressed="false">Your work</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="where" aria-pressed="false">Where you use it</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="prompts" aria-pressed="false">Prompts</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="agents" aria-pressed="false">Agents</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="trust" aria-pressed="false">Trust</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="org" aria-pressed="false">Your organisation</button>
          <span class="count" id="count" aria-live="polite"></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="list">
        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">A</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Action</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                An agent&rsquo;s ability to touch a real system rather than just talk about it &mdash; read a document
                library, send an email, add a row to a list, create a task. Without actions, an agent can only produce
                text.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>On screen it says:</b> tool. The Tools panel in Copilot Studio is worded &ldquo;connect the agent to external
                systems and actions&rdquo; &mdash; tool is the container, action is what it does. Separately, Copilot Actions
                is Microsoft&rsquo;s name for Copilot filling in a form or working through a website on your behalf: a feature,
                not a building block.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/fundamentals-what-is-copilot-studio"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Studio overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Agent</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                A Copilot you have given a name, a job, a set of rules and a fixed set of sources. Instead of explaining
                the task every time, you explain it once and then talk to the thing you made. Colleagues can use it too.
                Most agents wait to be asked; some are given their own account and work on a process without being
                prompted.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as:</b> Agent Mode, which is ordinary Copilot working harder inside one document. An agent
                you build by describing it is a declarative agent; one a developer writes in code is a custom-engine agent.
                You will only meet the first kind.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/fundamentals-what-is-copilot-studio"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Studio overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="where">
              <h3>Agent Mode</h3><span class="tag">Where you use it</span>
              <p>
                Copilot taking on a whole multi-step job inside Word, Excel or PowerPoint &mdash; building a document or
                reworking a spreadsheet across several moves &mdash; rather than making one edit and stopping. Generally
                available in those three apps.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/get-started-with-agent-mode-in-word-excel-and-powerpoint-4d322d7f-5e89-4f66-9fa4-57d328b156ff"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Get started with Agent Mode</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Agent Store</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                Where ready-made agents are published &mdash; Microsoft&rsquo;s, trusted partners&rsquo;, and your own
                organisation&rsquo;s &mdash; reachable from Teams, Outlook, Word, Excel and PowerPoint. Worth a look
                before you build anything: somebody has often done it already. Anything your colleagues build has to be
                approved by an admin before it appears there.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/copilot-agent-store"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Agent Store</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Analyst</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                A specialist agent that works with data. Give it a spreadsheet or a set of figures and it reasons
                through the analysis, writes and runs the code, and shows you its working. Needs the paid seat, and at
                launch Microsoft capped it and Researcher at 25 combined runs a month between them.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/blog/2025/06/02/researcher-and-analyst-are-now-generally-available-in-microsoft-365-copilot/"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Researcher and Analyst general availability</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Approval step</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                A point in a workflow where a person has to say yes before anything continues. Also called human in the
                loop. The safety catch on automation, and usually the difference between a workflow you can defend and
                one you cannot.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/workflows-experience/flows-overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Workflows overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Audit trail</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                The record of what Copilot was asked and what it did, kept in your organisation&rsquo;s compliance
                tools. It exists, and it matters for FOI, data-protection requests and investigations.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/microsoft-365-copilot-architecture-data-protection-auditing"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Data protection and auditing</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">C</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Chat history</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Your past conversations, listed in the chat pane. You can delete a single chat from its More menu, or
                wipe the lot from My Account under Data and Privacy. Deleting your copy is not the end of it: your
                organisation can apply a retention policy that keeps prompts and responses for compliance regardless.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as:</b> Memory. History is the transcript; memory is what Copilot concluded about how you
                work. Switch off chat-history personalisation and those conclusions are deleted &mdash; switch it back on
                within 30 days and they return.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365-copilot/how-microsoft-365-copilot-chat-history-works"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">How chat history works</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="work">
              <h3>Connector</h3><span class="tag">Your work</span>
              <p>
                A bridge that lets Copilot read a system that is not Microsoft&rsquo;s &mdash; a student record system,
                a CRM, a ticketing tool. Someone in IT sets these up; you only notice that Copilot suddenly knows more.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/connectors/"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot connectors</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licences">
              <h3>Copilot Academy</h3><span class="tag">Licences</span>
              <p>
                Guided Copilot training already switched on inside Viva Learning for most Microsoft 365 users. Free,
                already paid for, and usually nobody knows it is there.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/viva/learning/academy-copilot"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Academy</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licences">
              <h3>Copilot Credits</h3><span class="tag">Licences</span>
              <p>
                The metered currency for AI that sits outside the seat. Two things consume them. First, people without a
                paid seat using Copilot Chat, SharePoint agents or the retrieval API on a pay-as-you-go meter. Second,
                anything built on Copilot Studio&rsquo;s reasoning harness &mdash; and there billing starts the moment
                you start building, not when you publish: creating, previewing, testing and evaluating an agent all
                consume credits. Agents grounded only in their own instructions and public websites cost nothing.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Replaces:</b> the phrase &ldquo;Copilot Premium&rdquo;, which is not a Microsoft product. There are three
                things to buy: Copilot Chat (free), the Microsoft Copilot seat, and credits.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/agents-experience/billing-credit-overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Usage-based billing for agents</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="where">
              <h3>Copilot Notebooks</h3><span class="tag">Where you use it</span>
              <p>
                A place to pin the sources for one project &mdash; a set of documents, pages and notes &mdash; so that
                every question you ask is answered from those, and only those. The fix for &ldquo;it keeps dragging in
                the wrong report&rdquo;. Needs a Copilot or Copilot Chat licence.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/loop/cpcn-requirements"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Pages and Notebooks</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="where">
              <h3>Copilot Pages</h3><span class="tag">Where you use it</span>
              <p>
                Turns an answer in a chat into a document you and colleagues can edit together. Use it the moment an
                answer is worth keeping; chat threads are disposable, pages are not. Available on the free tier too
                &mdash; it needs a OneDrive licence rather than a Copilot one.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/loop/cpcn-requirements"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Pages and Notebooks</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="where">
              <h3>Copilot Search</h3><span class="tag">Where you use it</span>
              <p>
                Search across your organisation&rsquo;s content and connected third-party systems that answers the
                question rather than handing you twenty links to open. It comes with the paid seat at no extra cost,
                appears as a tab in the Copilot app, and needs no setting up.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/microsoft-365-copilot-search"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Search</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Copilot Studio</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                The place where agents, workflows and agent flows are built, tested and published &mdash; without
                writing code. This is where a repeated prompt becomes something your team can use.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as:</b> Power Automate, which follows fixed rules. An agent built in Studio exercises judgement
                about what to do next.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/fundamentals-what-is-copilot-studio"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Studio overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licences">
              <h3>Cowork</h3><span class="tag">Licences</span>
              <p>
                Long-running, delegated multi-step work: you give Copilot a task and walk away. The paid seat is a
                prerequisite, not an entitlement &mdash; holding Microsoft Copilot licences does not cover Cowork. Tasks
                are metered in Copilot Credits on top of the seat.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as:</b> Agent Mode, which works inside one document while you watch. Cowork is billed separately;
                the seat does not pay for it.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/usage-based-billing-overview-copilot-credits"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Usage-based billing and cost management for Copilot Credits</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Custom instructions</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                Standing context you set once &mdash; your role, your department, how you like things written &mdash; so
                you stop retyping it in every prompt.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as:</b> the Instructions you write when building an agent. Custom instructions shape your
                own Copilot; an agent&rsquo;s instructions shape everybody&rsquo;s.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/Microsoft-365-Copilot/personalize-what-microsoft-365-copilot-remembers"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Personalise what Copilot remembers</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">D</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Deep reasoning</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                Copilot taking longer to think before it answers. Slower and more expensive, and worth it for genuine
                analysis rather than for drafting an email.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/researcher-agent"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Researcher and Analyst</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="org">
              <h3>Disclosure</h3><span class="tag">Your organisation</span>
              <p>
                Saying that you used AI, and how. Your institution decides when this is required &mdash; marking,
                admissions, research outputs, student-facing writing &mdash; and the rule is usually about the decision,
                not the draft. Nobody declares spellcheck; people do declare a marking rubric applied by a machine. Find
                your own policy before you need it.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Our term:</b> not Microsoft&rsquo;s. Microsoft governs what the tool may do; your institution governs
                what you must say about it.
              </p>
              <p class="src">No Microsoft page defines this. It is a WorkSmart-AI teaching term.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>DLP</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Data loss prevention &mdash; the rules your organisation sets about what can leave, and where. Copilot
                obeys them, which is occasionally why an answer stops short.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/dlp-learn-about-dlp"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Purview data loss prevention</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">E</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licences">
              <h3>Education licences</h3><span class="tag">Licences</span>
              <p>
                The academic versions: Microsoft 365 A1, A3 and A5, and their Office 365 equivalents. Copilot Chat comes
                with them at no extra cost, though admins have to take extra steps before students aged 13 and over can
                use it. The paid Microsoft Copilot seat is a separate academic add-on, bought through EES or a reseller.
                &ldquo;We have A5&rdquo; therefore does not mean &ldquo;we have Copilot&rdquo;.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/microsoft-365-copilot-licensing"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Licence options for Microsoft Copilot</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Enterprise data protection</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                The contractual promise covering what you type into Copilot: prompts and responses carry the same terms
                as your mail in Exchange and your files in SharePoint, and are not used to train the foundation models.
                It applies to anyone signed in with a work account, with no admin action required, and it brings your
                sensitivity labels, retention policies and audit with it.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Older material calls this:</b> commercial data protection. For Copilot and Copilot Chat on a work account,
                the current term is enterprise data protection, or EDP.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/enterprise-data-protection"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Enterprise data protection</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">G</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="work">
              <h3>Grounding</h3><span class="tag">Your work</span>
              <p>
                Answering from real sources rather than from memory. A grounded answer carries numbered references you
                can click; an ungrounded one is the model&rsquo;s best guess. Look for the references before you trust
                the paragraph.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftsearch/semantic-index-for-copilot"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Semantic index for Copilot</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">H</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Hallucination</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                A confident, fluent, wrong answer. It is not a bug being fixed next release &mdash; it is how these
                tools work, and it is why verification is your job rather than Microsoft&rsquo;s.
              </p>
              <p class="src">No Microsoft page defines this. It is a WorkSmart-AI teaching term.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Harness</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                Which engine an agent runs on. Copilot Studio offers the GitHub Copilot harness for reasoning-heavy,
                multi-step work, the standard harness for rule-based conversations, and the Copilot chat harness for
                adding your knowledge to Copilot Chat. The choice decides how capable the agent is and how it is billed,
                so it is a budget decision as much as a technical one.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/harnesses-overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Agent harnesses overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">I</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Instructions</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                What you write when you build an agent: its job, its rules, its tone, what it must never do. The single
                biggest lever on whether the agent is any good.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/fundamentals-what-is-copilot-studio"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Studio overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Iteration</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                Steering the answer with follow-up turns instead of retyping the prompt from scratch.
                &ldquo;Shorter.&rdquo; &ldquo;More formal.&rdquo; &ldquo;Now for the student audience.&rdquo; The
                cheapest skill on this list and the most under-used.
              </p>
              <p class="src">No Microsoft page defines this. It is a WorkSmart-AI teaching term.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">K</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="work">
              <h3>Knowledge source</h3><span class="tag">Your work</span>
              <p>
                The specific places an agent is allowed to read &mdash; a SharePoint site, a set of documents, a
                connector. Narrowing these is what turns a vague agent into a reliable one.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/knowledge-copilot-studio"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Knowledge in Copilot Studio</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">M</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="where">
              <h3>Meeting recap</h3><span class="tag">Where you use it</span>
              <p>
                The summary, action points and &ldquo;what did I miss&rdquo; answers after a Teams meeting. The catch
                nobody mentions is the transcript. The organiser&rsquo;s meeting options decide it: set to only during
                the meeting, Copilot works live on temporary speech-to-text and leaves nothing behind; set to during and
                after, somebody has to actually start transcription. No transcript, no recap &mdash; and no asking about
                it tomorrow.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Also:</b> Copilot in meetings needs a paid seat, and does not work at all in end-to-end encrypted meetings.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/copilot-teams-transcription"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot in Teams meetings and transcription</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="work">
              <h3>Memory</h3><span class="tag">Your work</span>
              <p>
                Copilot holding on to preferences and context across sessions &mdash; how you like things written, what
                you work on, the tasks that recur &mdash; so it does not start from nothing each morning. You can read
                what it has saved in Chat settings, delete any of it, or turn the whole thing off. Turning it back on
                within 30 days restores what was deleted.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/Microsoft-365-Copilot/personalize-what-microsoft-365-copilot-remembers"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Personalise what Copilot remembers</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licences">
              <h3>Microsoft Copilot</h3><span class="tag">Licences</span>
              <p>
                The paid seat. This is the one that can see your work &mdash; files, mail, chats, meetings &mdash; and
                that appears inside Word, Excel, Outlook and Teams. Microsoft Copilot Business is the same idea sold
                against the Microsoft 365 Business plans.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Was called:</b> Microsoft 365 Copilot. Microsoft renamed it, and its own documentation says some experiences
                and licences will keep the old name during the transition.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/microsoft-365-copilot-licensing"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Licence options for Microsoft Copilot</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licences">
              <h3>Microsoft Copilot Chat</h3><span class="tag">Licences</span>
              <p>
                The free tier, included with any eligible Microsoft 365 subscription and reached at m365copilot.com. It
                has two halves: web-based chat, grounded in the internet and available to everyone, and work-based chat,
                which reads your organisation&rsquo;s content and needs the paid seat. Both carry enterprise data
                protection. File upload, image generation and Copilot Pages are included.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Why it matters:</b> the free and paid versions look almost identical on screen. Most &ldquo;Copilot is
                useless&rdquo; verdicts are somebody using the free one and expecting the demo.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Chat overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Model</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                The engine underneath. Copilot is not one fixed thing: it runs on frontier models from OpenAI and
                Anthropic, Microsoft swaps the default without telling you, and in some apps you can pick. An agent you
                build in Copilot Studio has its own model box at the top of the Build pane. The practical consequence is
                that the same prompt can behave differently this month than last, which is a reason to check output
                rather than to memorise tricks.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as:</b> the app. Copilot is Microsoft&rsquo;s wrapper &mdash; the licence, the permissions,
                the grounding &mdash; around somebody else&rsquo;s model.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/copilot-anthropic-apps"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot with Anthropic models</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Multi-agent orchestration</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                Agents handing work to other agents. On screen it is the Connected agents box in the Build pane. Real,
                occasionally useful, and firmly a job for whoever owns the platform rather than for you on a Tuesday
                afternoon.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/agents-experience/overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Build an agent</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">O</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Oversharing</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Copilot surfacing something that was always technically visible to you but practically buried &mdash; an
                old pay file in a site nobody tidied. Copilot did not break a permission. It found one that was already
                wrong. IT can hide a site from Copilot and organisation-wide search without changing who may open it,
                using restricted content discovery, which also strips the Copilot buttons from that site.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/restricted-content-discovery"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Restricted content discovery</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">P</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Permissions inheritance</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Copilot can only see what your account can already see &mdash; no more, and no less. The answer to
                &ldquo;can it read my colleague&rsquo;s mail?&rdquo; is no, unless you already could.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/microsoft-365-copilot-architecture-data-protection-auditing"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Data protection and auditing</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Prompt</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                What you ask for. A good one carries five things: the goal, the context, the source to use, the format
                you want back, and the tone. Most disappointing answers are missing three of those.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/learn-about-copilot-prompts-f6c3b467-f07c-4db1-ae54-ffac96184dd5"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Learn about Copilot prompts</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Prompt Gallery</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                A library of prompts inside Copilot &mdash; Microsoft&rsquo;s, your organisation&rsquo;s, and ones you
                save yourself. Start here rather than from a blank box.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/copilot-prompt-gallery"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Prompt Gallery</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">R</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Referencing</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                Pointing Copilot at a specific file, person or meeting inside your prompt, usually by typing a slash or
                an oblique. Turns &ldquo;summarise our policy&rdquo; into a question it can actually answer.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/learn-about-copilot-prompts-f6c3b467-f07c-4db1-ae54-ffac96184dd5"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Learn about Copilot prompts</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Researcher</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                A specialist agent that works through a question over several steps, across your work data and the web,
                and comes back with a sourced report rather than a paragraph. It deliberately takes longer, and it will
                ask you clarifying questions on the way. Needs the paid seat, and shares a monthly allowance of 25 runs
                with Analyst.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/blog/2025/06/02/researcher-and-analyst-are-now-generally-available-in-microsoft-365-copilot/"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Researcher and Analyst general availability</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Responsible use</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Your institution&rsquo;s own rules about what AI may be used for &mdash; marking, admissions, personal
                data, student-facing decisions. These sit above anything Microsoft permits, and they are the ones you
                will be held to.
              </p>
              <p class="src">No Microsoft page defines this. It is a WorkSmart-AI teaching term.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">S</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Saved and team prompts</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                A prompt you keep, and one your team shares. The step between &ldquo;I worked out a good prompt&rdquo;
                and &ldquo;the department has a good prompt&rdquo;.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/copilot-prompt-gallery"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Copilot Prompt Gallery</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Sensitivity label</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                The classification stuck to a document &mdash; public, internal, confidential. Copilot and agents
                recognise it. Where an answer draws on several documents, you see the most restrictive label of the
                sources, and where a label encrypts the file, Copilot returns its content only if you personally have
                the right to copy from it.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/sensitivity-labels"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Sensitivity labels</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>SharePoint agent</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                An agent created straight from a site or document library, so it answers only from what is in there, and
                only what each person is already allowed to see. The easiest first agent anyone builds, and often the
                only one a team needs. You need a paid seat to create one; colleagues without a seat can use it only if
                your organisation has switched on pay-as-you-go billing.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/get-started-sharepoint-agents"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Agents in SharePoint</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Skill</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                One named thing an agent can do, written out once: &ldquo;triage an enquiry&rdquo;, &ldquo;draft a reply
                in our house style&rdquo;, &ldquo;flag anything about fees&rdquo;. In Copilot Studio a skill is three
                fields &mdash; a name, a description of when it applies, and the instructions to follow &mdash; and the
                agent picks the one whose description matches what it has been asked. An agent with three clear skills
                beats an agent with one vague instruction.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as:</b> a tool. In the Build pane, Skills sits directly above Tools: a skill is behaviour,
                how to do the job; a tool is reach, the system it touches. Skills are plain Markdown, so a good one can be
                handed to another agent.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/agents-experience/skills-overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Skills overview for agents</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">T</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="org">
              <h3>Tacit knowledge</h3><span class="tag">Your organisation</span>
              <p>
                What your team knows but has never written down &mdash; so Copilot cannot read it. The most common
                reason a technically correct answer is still the wrong answer for your institution.
              </p>
              <p class="src">No Microsoft page defines this. It is a WorkSmart-AI teaching term.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Tenant</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Your organisation&rsquo;s own walled section of Microsoft&rsquo;s cloud. &ldquo;Inside the tenant&rdquo;
                means inside the boundary your data protection promise covers.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/enterprise-data-protection"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Enterprise data protection</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Trigger</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                What starts a workflow without you: a time of day, an email arriving, a file being added, a form being
                submitted. Choosing the trigger is most of designing the workflow.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/workflows-experience/flows-overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Workflows overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">V</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Verification</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                Checking the output before you use it. This does not transfer to the tool, the vendor or your IT
                department. If it goes out under your name, it is yours.
              </p>
              <p class="src">No Microsoft page defines this. It is a WorkSmart-AI teaching term.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">W</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="work">
              <h3>What Copilot cannot see</h3><span class="tag">Your work</span>
              <p>
                The honest limits of the organisation-wide index. It covers Word, PowerPoint, PDF, OneNote and
                SharePoint pages, plus your own mailbox. It does not cover shared or delegated mailboxes, archived
                content, or anything in a site an admin has set not to appear in search. Files over 512 MB are skipped,
                and images and video are ignored beyond their file names. Spreadsheets are not in the indexed list,
                which is why &ldquo;find the report&rdquo; works better than &ldquo;find the spreadsheet&rdquo;.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Also:</b> a new document on a SharePoint site is indexed daily rather than instantly, so a file added
                this morning may genuinely not be findable this afternoon.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftsearch/semantic-index-for-copilot"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Semantic indexing for Copilot</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="where">
              <h3>Where Copilot lives</h3><span class="tag">Where you use it</span>
              <p>
                One product, several doors: the Microsoft Copilot app at m365.cloud.microsoft, the side pane inside
                Word, Excel, Outlook and Teams, the Copilot button on a SharePoint library, and Edge. What you can do
                changes with the room you are standing in &mdash; the app is for asking across everything, the side pane
                is for working on the thing in front of you.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Note:</b> the free Copilot Chat lives at m365copilot.com and redirects into the same app. The consumer
                Copilot at copilot.microsoft.com is a different product on a personal account.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/which-copilot-for-your-organization"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Decide which Copilot is right for you</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="work">
              <h3>Work IQ</h3><span class="tag">Your work</span>
              <p>
                Microsoft&rsquo;s name for the layer that gives Copilot an understanding of your organisation &mdash;
                your documents, mail, meetings, and how people and projects connect, built on the plumbing IT calls the
                Microsoft Graph. It is the reason Copilot&rsquo;s answer is different from a public chatbot&rsquo;s, and
                the reason it can be wrong in specific, local ways.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>What it is not:</b> a person reading your email. It is a permissions-bound index your account already
                had access to.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/extensibility/work-iq/"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Work IQ overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="prompts">
              <h3>Work, web, or both</h3><span class="tag">Prompts</span>
              <p>
                The toggle that decides whether Copilot answers from your organisation&rsquo;s content, from the open
                internet, or from both. It changes the answer completely, and people forget it is there.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>On screen it says:</b> Work IQ. Turning that button on grounds the answer in what your account can reach;
                turning it off sends you back to the web.
              </p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/copilot/which-copilot-for-your-organization"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Decide which Copilot is right for you</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="agents">
              <h3>Workflow</h3><span class="tag">Agents</span>
              <p>
                An ordered set of steps that runs on a trigger, usually without you watching. A step can call an agent,
                wait for a person to approve, and then carry on. Copilot Studio also keeps the older, more literal
                version under the name agent flows.
              </p>
              <p class="not"><b>Not the same as:</b> an agent. The agent is the worker; the workflow is the rota.</p>
              <p class="src">
                Microsoft: <a
                  href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot-studio/workflows-experience/flows-overview"
                  rel="nofollow noopener"
                  target="_blank">Workflows overview</a
                >
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

      <p class="empty" id="empty">Nothing matches that. Try a shorter word, or clear the filter.</p>

      <p class="foot">
        <strong>A note on names.</strong> Microsoft renames things often, and this page carries the scars: its own documentation
        now says Microsoft Copilot where it used to say Microsoft 365 Copilot, and enterprise data protection where older
        material says commercial data protection. The four core terms &mdash; agent, skill, action, workflow &mdash; describe
        how the thing works and will outlast whatever it is called this quarter; all four appear on the Copilot Studio build
        screen. Where a name here does not match your screen, trust the screen and tell us. Every entry was checked against
        the linked Microsoft page on 20 August 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

